# NickelOS: what ChromeOS needs of Gentoo packages the OS takes
# (found by the image install, plan step 3).
# libbrillo: sys-fs/lvm2[thin]
sys-fs/lvm2 lvm thin
# Kept small: what Gentoo's defaults pull in that ChromeOS doesn't use
# (the image install, 2026-10-18): GCC through libb2's OpenMP, Qt through
# poppler and avahi, ffmpeg through localsearch, speech-dispatcher through brltty.
app-crypt/libb2 -openmp
app-text/poppler -qt6
net-dns/avahi -qt6
app-misc/localsearch gstreamer
app-accessibility/brltty -speech -X -python -tcl -ocamlopt -lua -java -emacs
# The Terminal's vim (install.txt) without the desktop extras ChromeOS
# doesn't have: gpm, libcanberra (sound), libsodium (encrypted files), the
# X11 and Wayland clipboard; the Terminal runs in Chrome.
app-editors/vim -X -crypt -gpm -pango -sound -wayland
# SELinux where Google builds with it (ChromeOS's policy is enforcing: file
# contexts kept and shown, chcon and runcon): Gentoo masks the flag outside
# its SELinux profiles, so it is unmasked for these (profile/package.use.mask).
# Not coreutils' multicall build (Google's): Gentoo's makes each command a
# script that renames itself with prctl(PR_SET_MM), which ChromeOS's
# sandboxes kill (the installer's chromeos-install, for one).
app-arch/tar selinux
sys-apps/coreutils selinux
sys-apps/findutils selinux
sys-process/lsof selinux
sys-process/procps selinux
# OpenRC runs your services (sshd, CUPS, Samba) under ChromeOS's init:
# Upstart is init, and shill runs the network (docs/unified.md, OpenRC).
sys-apps/openrc -sysvinit -netifrc
# ecryptfs-utils without its GTK front-end, as Google builds it (it pulled in
# GTK 2 and an at-spi2-core with introspection, which NickelOS's newer one
# isn't). Its TPM key module (Google's tpm flag) doesn't build with GCC 16,
# and cryptohome hands ecryptfs its own keys.
sys-fs/ecryptfs-utils -gtk
# perf without its GTK and terminal front-ends, closer to Google's (crypt
# zlib): the GTK one pulled in GTK 2.
dev-util/perf -gtk -slang
# /bin/sh is dash, as ChromeOS's init scripts are written for: Gentoo's
# app-alternatives/sh (in @system, so a world update installs it) points it
# at bash by default.
app-alternatives/sh -bash dash
# One Mesa (vision decision 5), Gentoo's, for Chrome and Linux apps: VA-API
# for Chrome's video decoding (AMD's comes from Mesa), virgl for the VM's GPU.
media-libs/mesa vaapi video_cards_virgl
# Where NickelOS keeps Gentoo's flag over Google's (zz-unified-google,
# read before this, gives the OS's copies Google's flags):
# - coreutils not multicall (above).
# - Portage verifies Gentoo's tree when you sync it; ChromeOS never syncs.
# - systemd-utils' udev: its 32-bit libudev is Steam's (the image's udev
#   stays Google's sys-fs/udev).
# - ecryptfs-utils' TPM module doesn't build with GCC 16 (above).
# - Gentoo's ebuild needs a flag with one of Google's (REQUIRED_USE):
#   iputils' filecaps needs caps; Gentoo's newer pango needs freetype's
#   harfbuzz; ffmpeg's dvd (newer than Google's ebuild) needs gpl, and
#   Google's ffmpeg is LGPL.
# - Gentoo's other packages need them (the ones built for your binhost:
#   GTK, Qt, WebKitGTK, fwupd's capsule splash, SELinux's Python tools,
#   brltty): typelibs, Python bindings, libxkbcommon's X11 library,
#   tiff's JPEG. Nothing a ChromeOS service runs.
sys-apps/coreutils -multicall
sys-apps/portage rsync-verify
sys-apps/systemd-utils udev
sys-fs/ecryptfs-utils -tpm
net-misc/iputils caps
media-libs/freetype harfbuzz
media-video/ffmpeg -dvd
media-libs/tiff jpeg
x11-libs/gdk-pixbuf introspection
x11-libs/pango introspection
media-libs/harfbuzz icu introspection
app-crypt/gnupg alternatives
sys-libs/libselinux python
sys-process/audit python
dev-python/pygobject cairo
dev-libs/json-glib introspection
x11-libs/libxkbcommon X
dev-libs/libpcre2 pcre32
# xdg-user-dir, which Steam and other apps ask for the desktop folder; without
# xdg-user-dirs-gtk, a dialog that offers to rename the folders after a
# language change (NickelOS keeps them as they are: nickel-session).
x11-misc/xdg-user-dirs -gtk
